"Groups repeated seasonal positions into separate mini time series to reveal both seasonal differences and within-season trends.\n\nUse when: Several years of regular observations contain a known seasonal cycle. Readers need to compare typical months while inspecting each month across years.\n\nAvoid when: The sampling is irregular or there are too few complete seasonal cycles.\n\nVisual grammar: Group position identifies the seasonal slot such as month. Within-group order encodes successive years, while vertical position encodes the observed value.\n\nBuild: Choose a seasonal period supported by the measurement process. Split observations by seasonal position and retain chronological order within each group. Draw each subseries with an optional seasonal mean rule and concise labels.\n\nCheck: Check observations are assigned to correct seasons and years. Verify each group uses the same vertical scale. Identify incomplete cycles instead of silently treating them as complete.\n\nPitfalls: Connecting different seasonal groups implies a continuity the layout does not have. Long-term trend can inflate apparent seasonal differences; inspect a detrended view when appropriate.\n\nExample: Inspect synthetic monthly energy use over eight years, grouping January observations together through December.\n\nAlternatives: Seasonal overlay plot, Calendar heatmap, Line chart."
